vo_avfoundation: give tvOS frames after a seek or resume their subtitles and lead

After a seek mpv flips the first frames back to back as they decode, and
the render thread falls behind them. The third and fourth frames after a
seek then went to the layers without their subtitles: the third's wait
deadline (half its queue lead) had passed, and the fourth did not wait
because the render thread was still on an older frame. Their picture
showed with the subtitle of two frames before, for about 170 ms
(plezy#3073). At a resume the first frame reached the layers 2.9-3.0
refreshes before its refresh, where the video layer shows it a refresh
late and the OSD layer does not: its subtitle showed a refresh before its
picture.

On tvOS, each of the first eight timed frames after the start, a seek or
a resume waits for its own subtitles whether or not the render thread is
behind, as long as its deadline allows and at least 25 ms, until one such
wait runs out. A frame whose refresh is then less than 3.5 refreshes away
is stamped whole refreshes later until it is not, before its stamp is
first used: when a deferred subtitle change is filed with it
(osd-queue-lead-ms), or else right before its samples are made. The
frames after it follow it as any frame does (avf_host_present_ns), so a
moved frame can share a refresh with the next one, which then shows in
its place. Steady playback is not moved. Where no refresh is known
(elsewhere than tvOS) nothing changes.

vo.c told the driver of a pause or a resume after the frame it drew in
the same iteration, so the first frame after an unpause could be drawn
before the resume, as if still paused. The driver is now told under the
lock that picks the frame, before drawing it. In media mode (iOS, macOS)
the first frame after a resume now always anchors the timebase at its own
time, as it already did when the resume came first. The other drivers
only ask for a redraw at a pause, which they now get an iteration sooner.

Apple TV 4K (3rd generation), Plezy's options, the mode switch made after
the first frame; clips whose frames and per-frame ASS events carry the
same code, read from an HDMI capture; 24 Hz unless said. Before this patch
(0054 and 0055), and with it:

- After a seek, a subtitle other than the picture's on 2 or more
  captures: 12 of 136 seeks before, none of 136 with it; the third and
  fourth frames both without their subtitles: 18 before, none.
- After an unpause, any capture with a subtitle other than the picture's:
  2 of 39 before, none of 190; at playback start 7 of 9, none of 15.
- Steady playback: none of 8964 captures mismatched before, none of 13243.
- With 120 blurred signs on screen (renders 16 ms, p99 35-58 ms): after a
  seek 50 of 58 before, none of 96; after an unpause 4 of 45, none of 70.
- 25p at 50 Hz (40 seeks), 23.976 and 29.97 at 60 Hz (40 seeks), HDR10
  (38 seeks, 20 unpauses) and Dolby Vision 8.1 shown as HDR10 (20 seeks):
  no mismatched steady capture, seek or unpause; 80 frame steps, none.
- Of 4904 frames after a seek or a resume, 3 (with the 120 signs) reached
  the layers less than 3.5 refreshes before their refresh, the least 3.41.
  At a resume about one frame was moved, mostly by one refresh.

Left as they were: the first picture after a seek can show for a refresh
before its subtitle (53 of 136 seeks; 89 before), and with heavy
typesetting the first frames of a file can go without their subtitles
while the first render runs.

